    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya.jpg" alt="Bodh Gaya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahabodhi_temple_at_Bodhgaya_in_Bihar_21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amitabha Gupta, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buddhism began with the life and teaching of Siddhartha Gautama, a member of the Shakya clan who was born, according to tradition, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a> in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. His dates are uncertain. Buddhist traditions and older scholarship place his life in the 6th and early 5th centuries BCE; many historians now prefer a later reckoning, with his death around 400 BCE. All agree that he lived and taught in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and Kosala, at a time of growing cities, new states and lively debate among wandering ascetics about the nature of the self and the way to release from rebirth.</p><p>The traditional account tells how the young prince, raised in comfort, encountered old age, sickness and death, left his home and family, and spent years in severe ascetic practice before rejecting it as fruitless. Seated in meditation beneath a fig tree at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, he attained awakening and became the Buddha, the “awakened one”. He gave his first teaching to five former companions in the deer park at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, an event remembered as the first turning of the wheel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>.</p><p>For some forty-five years he taught across the Ganges valley, gathering a community, the Sangha, of monks and later nuns, supported by lay followers who included merchants and kings. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushinagar">Kushinagar</a> at about eighty years of age. Buddhists call his death the parinirvana, his final release.</p><p>The early tradition was preserved orally. According to Buddhist accounts, his disciples met in council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a> soon after his death to recite his teachings and the rules of the order, and a second council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> about a century later dealt with disputes over monastic discipline. Disagreements of this kind led to the first divisions of the community into schools. The teaching itself was carried in the vernacular languages of north India, among them the forms of Middle Indo-Aryan from which the scriptural language Pali derives.</p><p>Buddhism was one of several movements of the time that rejected the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and the sacrificial ritual of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests. Its closest contemporary rival was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, taught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, which shared much of its vocabulary and its rejection of animal sacrifice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha.jpg" alt="Gautama Buddha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_(Dhammajak_Mutra).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">พระมหาเทวประภาส วชิรญาณเมธี (ผู้ถ่าย-ปล่อยสัญญาอนุญาตภาพให้นำไปใช้ได้เพื่อการศึกษาโดยอยู่ภา่ยใต้ cc-by-sa-3.0) ผู้สร้..., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the heart of the Buddha’s teaching, as the early texts present it, are the Four Noble Truths: life is marked by suffering or unsatisfactoriness (dukkha); suffering arises from craving; craving can cease; and there is a path that leads to its cessation. That path is the Noble Eightfold Path of right view, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness and concentration, usually grouped as ethics, meditation and wisdom.</p><p>Buddhism shares with other Indian traditions the belief in rebirth governed by karma, the moral consequences of intentional action. Its distinctive claim is that there is no permanent, unchanging self (anatman) that passes from life to life: a person is a changing stream of physical and mental processes. All conditioned things are impermanent, and clinging to them brings suffering. The goal is nirvana, the extinction of craving, hatred and delusion and release from the cycle of rebirth.</p><p>Practice combines ethical precepts with meditation. Lay Buddhists undertake five precepts: to refrain from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech and intoxicants. Monks and nuns follow the far longer rules of the Vinaya. Meditation trains calm and concentration and cultivates insight into impermanence and non-self. Devotion has also been central from early times: the veneration of the Buddha’s relics in stupas, pilgrimage to the places of his life, offerings of flowers and lamps, and the recitation of texts.</p><p>Later traditions developed these ideas in different directions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a>, which emerged around the turn of the Common Era, made its ideal the bodhisattva, who seeks awakening for the sake of all beings, and taught that all phenomena are empty (shunyata) of fixed essence. It recognised many Buddhas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">bodhisattvas</a>, among them Amitabha, whose Pure Land became a focus of devotion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and Avalokiteshvara, the bodhisattva of compassion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a>, which arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about the 6th century, added tantric rituals, mantras and visualisation practices taught by a guru.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> countries, and increasingly elsewhere, the full moon of Vesak, in April or May, marks the Buddha’s birth, awakening and death together; East Asian traditions celebrate these events on separate days.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana.jpg" alt="Tripitaka Koreana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana">Tripitaka Koreana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripitaka_Koreana_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buddha’s teachings were transmitted orally for several centuries. The oldest complete collection to survive is the Pali Canon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> tradition, known as the Tipitaka or “three baskets”: the Vinaya, the rules of monastic life; the Sutta Pitaka, the discourses of the Buddha; and the Abhidhamma, systematic analyses of mind and experience. According to the Sri Lankan chronicles, the canon was written down at the Aluvihara monastery in the 1st century BCE, when war and famine threatened the monks who had memorised it.</p><p>Other early schools kept their own canons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and other Indian languages; much of this survives only in Chinese and Tibetan translation, or in fragments such as the birch-bark scrolls from Gandhara, which date from the 1st century CE and are among the oldest Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> known.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> produced a large new literature of sutras presented as the Buddha’s words, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita">Perfection of Wisdom sutras</a>, including the Diamond Sutra and the Heart Sutra, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> and the Pure Land sutras. Philosophers systematised its ideas. Nagarjuna, in the 2nd or 3rd century CE, founded the Madhyamaka, or “middle way”, school with his analysis of emptiness; the brothers Asanga and Vasubandhu, in the 4th or 5th century, shaped the Yogacara school, which explored the workings of consciousness. Buddhaghosa, working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in the 5th century, wrote the Visuddhimagga and commentaries that defined Theravada orthodoxy.</p><p>Translation carried these texts across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a>, who arrived in Chang’an in 401, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 645 with hundreds of manuscripts, produced Chinese versions that remain standard. The Chinese canon was printed in its entirety in the 10th century, and the Korean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana">Tripitaka Koreana</a>, carved on more than 80,000 wooden blocks in the 13th century, is preserved at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haeinsa">Haeinsa</a>. Tibetan scholars translated the Indian texts into the two great collections of the Kangyur and Tengyur.</p><p>Teachers in later centuries added their own classics, among them the Japanese monks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a> and Dogen and the Tibetan scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borobudur.jpg" alt="Borobudur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pradaksina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heri nugroho, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buddhism’s first great patron was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, the Mauryan emperor of the 3rd century BCE, who after his bloody conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> turned to the Buddha’s teaching. His inscriptions, carved on rocks and pillars across the subcontinent, urge restraint, honesty and kindness to living beings. Buddhist tradition credits him with sending missions abroad, including his son Mahinda to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, which became a lasting centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a>.</p><p>From the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Buddhism spread with merchants and monks along the trade routes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. It flourished under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, whose territories in Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> produced a Buddhist art shaped by Greek and Roman models, and it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by the 1st century CE. Over the following centuries it became one of the great religions of China, adapting to Chinese culture and producing schools of its own such as Chan, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as Zen, and Pure Land.</p><p>From China it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in the 4th century and to Japan in the 6th, introduced from the Korean kingdom of Baekje in 538 or 552 and promoted by Prince Shotoku. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> received it in the 7th and 8th centuries; the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samye">Samye</a> was founded around 779, and Buddhism later became the foundation of Tibetan culture and government. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> traditions mingled in the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> was built around 800, while from the 11th century the Theravada, renewed from Sri Lanka, became the religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>.</p><p>In India itself Buddhism declined. Royal patronage shifted to Hindu devotional traditions, and the great monastic universities in the east, which depended on that patronage, were sacked during the Turkic conquests around 1200; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> was among them. By the 13th century Buddhism had largely disappeared from the land of its birth, surviving on its Himalayan fringes, while it continued to flourish in Sri Lanka and across the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><p>In modern times it returned to India and travelled west. Western scholars translated its texts in the 19th century; in 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, the jurist and campaigner for the rights of the Dalits, became a Buddhist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> with hundreds of thousands of followers; and in the 20th century Buddhist teachers and Asian migrants established communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nalanda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nalanda.jpg" alt="Nalanda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_No.-_3,_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Odantapuribs, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buddha’s community, the Sangha, was from the start a community of renunciants supported by the laity. Monks and nuns gave up property and family life, kept the rules of the Vinaya and depended on alms; lay followers gave food, robes and land, and in return received teaching and the merit that Buddhism teaches comes from generosity. This exchange of material support for spiritual guidance has shaped Buddhist societies ever since.</p><p>Monasteries grew into great institutions. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> attracted students from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> to study philosophy, grammar, logic and medicine; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> spent years there in the 7th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, monasteries became schools for village children, and young men commonly spent a period as novices. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, monasteries held land, ran charitable works and sometimes wielded political and military power; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, the great monasteries and their lamas became the core of the state, and from the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> held both religious and political authority.</p><p>Kings found in Buddhism a model of righteous rule, the “wheel-turning” monarch who governs by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>, and many supported the Sangha, purified it when they judged it lax, and gained legitimacy from it in turn. Buddhism also met opposition: Chinese emperors restricted monasteries several times, most severely in the persecution of 845, and in the 20th century communist governments in China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> suppressed it.</p><p>The order of nuns, founded according to tradition when the Buddha accepted his aunt and foster mother Mahaprajapati, flourished in India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, but the full ordination of women died out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> and was never established in Tibet; its revival has been a subject of debate and practice in recent decades.</p><p>Lay practice varies widely: offerings at temples, festivals, pilgrimages, meditation retreats, the chanting of texts and the observance of precepts on holy days. In East Asia, Buddhist rites have long been central to funerals and the remembrance of ancestors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan.jpg" alt="Buddhas of Bamiyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_of_Bamiyan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Gordon, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buddhism is one of the great religious traditions of the world and the source of much of the art, thought and literature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Its monuments include the stupa of Sanchi, the painted caves of Ajanta, the colossal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a>, destroyed by the Taliban in 2001, the temple-mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a>, the pagodas of Bagan and the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. Its images of the Buddha, first carved in human form in Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> in the early centuries CE, became one of the most widespread artistic forms in history.</p><p>Buddhist philosophy produced sophisticated theories of mind, perception and language, and Buddhist monasteries preserved and spread learning, medicine and the technologies of the written word; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> developed largely to reproduce Buddhist texts and charms. Chan and Zen shaped the poetry, painting, gardens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>In politics, Buddhism has inspired both quietism and activism. It has served as the religion of kingdoms and nations, from medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and Bhutan, and has been invoked in movements of national identity, sometimes in ways that have deepened conflict with religious minorities. The Tibetan tradition, led since 1959 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, has won wide sympathy abroad.</p><p>Since the 19th century Buddhist ideas have travelled far beyond Asia. Meditation practices adapted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> and Zen traditions underlie the modern secular practice of mindfulness, which entered medicine and psychology in the late 20th century. The ideals of non-violence, compassion and the impermanence of all things continue to give the tradition a wide appeal among people of many faiths and none.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Buddhism, founded on the teaching of Siddhartha Gautama in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 5th century BCE, offers a path of ethics, meditation and insight to end suffering. Carried across Asia by kings, monks and merchants, it shaped the art, learning and politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, faded from India after about 1200 and in modern times has spread worldwide.</em></p>

